<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hooge</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1609">1609</a>–<a href="/years/1648">1648</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Prince Su</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hooge was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> prince of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>, and held the title Prince Su. He commanded Qing armies in the conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and in 1646 led the campaign into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-xianzhong">Zhang Xianzhong</a>, ruler of the Great Xi regime, was killed in early 1647. On his return he fell foul of the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorgon">Dorgon</a>, was imprisoned and died in prison in 1648.</p></div>
